WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.970
No, I think we . . . We were very blessed that we didn’t have too many things to distract us.

2
00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:16.540
Go down my football team, my first team.

3
00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:23.680
Pete Burge, in the end, graduated on schedule. [Justin] Rukas graduated in Geology.

4
00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:28.440
Bert Brown graduated in Education.

5
00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:41.220
Moose [Marvin] Stewart graduated and went and became a colonel in the Marines . . . lieutenant colonel in the Marine Corps. [Osborne] “Butch” Helveston graduated in Education.

6
00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:44.780
He was Superintendent of Schools in Baton Rouge.

7
00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:53.740
“Jeff” Barrett graduated in Geology.

8
00:00:53.740 --> 00:01:04.080
Junior Bowman graduated in Geology. [Ernest “Son”] Seago graduated in Education. [Jesse] Fatheree graduated in Education.

9
00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:05.520
I graduated Pre-Med.

10
00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:14.520
I mean, we had a few, but certainly I would say our college graduation,

11
00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:24.420
I would just estimate would be in the eighty-five, ninety percent range because things were entirely different.

12
00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:25.840
Right.

13
00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:28.380
And you went there to get an education.

14
00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:38.760
Today, most athletes go to college to go to professional football, so the incentive is towards athletics and not towards education.

15
00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:52.560
It’s hard to compare and I don’t think we should compare the past with the present because we each have different obligations and different parameters that we had to live by or travel by,

16
00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:54.200
and it’s hard to say.

17
00:01:54.200 --> 00:02:01.320
Just like, a lot of people have asked me, “Do you think you could play in today’s football?”

18
00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:03.240
I don’t know.

19
00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:06.400
Things were different in my day.

20
00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:08.060
I weighed one hundred and eighty pounds.

21
00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:15.480
Today, the pros, they’d just pick you up with one hand, throw you away [laughs].

22
00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:18.760
But things were different.

23
00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:20.780
We played offense and defense.

24
00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:22.280
We had different equipment.

25
00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:27.100
When I had on my equipment, I weighed two hundred pounds.

26
00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:31.160
I had twenty pounds of equipment because we had leather.

27
00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:33.340
We didn’t have plastics.

28
00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:34.780
We didn’t have foam.

29
00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:40.980
We didn’t have a lot of things they have today, they make.

30
00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:44.240
We had high-top leather shoes, leather cleats.

31
00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:53.360
Today in the athletics, the football equipment would be eight and a half, nine pounds.

32
00:02:53.360 --> 00:03:03.100
In my day, it was eighteen to twenty pounds because of orthopedic felt and leather and . . . You perspired and the felt absorbed the moisture,

33
00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:04.940
so it got wet and heavy.

34
00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:09.540
You see, things were so different, it’s hard to compare.
